@ttada 
It could be happening because there's no other item in the listview to focus on, so the focus goes back to back button by default. Also, accessibility on iOS works differently from Android.
Can you try to add more items in the list view and then follow the steps to see if the focus moves to next item on the list or goes back to the top ?

@darshankawar
The same problem occurred when I implemented sorting with the number of items set to 10. The focus is not on back button, It is in the empty position above the back button. As a new discovery, the animated items seem to go wrong. For example, When I move the 4th item to the 6th item, the 4th, 5th, and 6th items will be incorrect. But when the 6th item is moved to the 4th, the 5th and 6th items will be incorrect.

https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/8084530/160595702-e7759ac4-7947-4a07-8559-c879dd61ef7d.MP4

I tried this on my iPad and it was more obvious.

https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/8084530/160597526-45e17b34-bb89-4740-9e3c-b1a3aba3a761.MP4

<details>
<summary>Reorder Code</summary>

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

void main() {
  runApp(const MyApp());
}

class MyApp extends StatelessWidget {
  const MyApp({Key? key}) : super(key: key);

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return MaterialApp(
      title: 'Reorder Test',
      theme: ThemeData(primarySwatch: Colors.blue),
      home: const MyHomePage(),
    );
  }
}

class MyHomePage extends StatefulWidget {
  const MyHomePage({Key? key}) : super(key: key);

  @override
  State<MyHomePage> createState() => _MyHomePageState();
}

class _MyHomePageState extends State<MyHomePage> {
  final _itemKeys = [for (var i = 0; i < 10; i++) ValueKey(i)];

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Scaffold(
      appBar: AppBar(
          leading: IconButton(
            icon: const Icon(Icons.arrow_back),
            onPressed: () {},
          ),
          title: const Text('Reorder Test')),
      body: ReorderableListView(
        children: [
          for (var key in _itemKeys)
            ListTile(
              key: key,
              title: Text('Item ${key.value}'),
            ),
        ],
        onReorder: (oldIndex, newIndex) {
          final item = _itemKeys.removeAt(oldIndex);
          _itemKeys.insert(newIndex - (newIndex > oldIndex ? 1 : 0), item);
          setState(() {});
        },
      ),
    );
  }
}
```
</details>
